WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.020
Do you get nostalgic ... Are you nostalgic when we talk about ABBA?

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.060
No, I can't actually say that I am.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
It wasn't like I was ...

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:10.660
But everyone else is!

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.740
It's not like I wish that I was still standing there -

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:17.420
- like Mick Jagger is.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:21.800
It was good while it lasted.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.060
So I look back on it with joy and gratitude.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:30.340
But would you, in theory, be able to?

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.800
No, I don't think so.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.520
If you for example ask Benny, he would say: «NO, that won't work»!

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:44.520
When you get questions about ABBA, do you have to ask the other members, or ...

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.740
It's enough that one of us says «no».

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:49.960
Questions like «may we use 'Dancing Queen' in this movie» -

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:52.780
Then we say «no».

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.700
There's a lot of questions like that.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.900
And then there's also quite a lot of interesting requests, someone who wants to create a game -

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.600
- whatever it may be called ... What was the name of the one recently?

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.020
«You can dance»?

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:06.180
«Singstar», I think it was called.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.460
Some kind of karaoke setup with our old videos.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.400
And that kind of thing can be quite cool. So then we said «yes»!

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.700
And if someone says «yes», we have to check with all four.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.400
And ask if everyone thinks it's okay.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:18.360
I wonder ...

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:24.620
When you look at Björn and Benny, they've both been here -

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.540
- and now you ... All three of you have got in common that you ...

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:32.320
You want to be ordinary.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:33.660
In some way.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:34.460
We ARE ordinary.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.980
Is Frida as ordinary as the rest of you?

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:42.980
Yes. I think so.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.080
So the four of you are the same level of ordinary?

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:48.240
Yes, one could say that. We're the same level of boring!

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.000
If we go back in time -

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:53.940
- was one of you the boss?

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.500
I would say that all of us were the boss.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.960
We were very strong people, all of us.

00:01:58.965 --> 00:02:00.145
And were will strong.

00:02:00.145 --> 00:02:02.875
And I think that's the reason it worked out so well.

00:02:03.455 --> 00:02:05.135
Who would buckle first?

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.320
It certainly wasn't me!

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:18.115
We really enjoyed ourselves because we all -

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.760
- were gifted and we all had something to contribute with.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.160
And I had quite a lot of -

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.100
- ideas about choruses and little -

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:32.960
- inserts here and there so we could make the songs a little more -

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:37.080
- alive, in a way. Like a little gimmick in every song.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.660
So that was probably my contribution.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:43.800
And people have asked why I didn't write more material for ABBA but -

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:49.140
- I also had periods where I felt that «God, it's so nice to be home».

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:52.260
We were traveling a lot, and I had two small children at home -

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.460
- and when I came home I just felt that -

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:58.060
I wanted to take care of that. I went from ...

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:00.440
... a luxury suite to -

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:02.400
- doing the dishes -

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.380
- and I thought it was wonderful!

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:06.020
You were born after ABBA?

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:06.780
Yes.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.020
It was my fault!

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:09.320
What does ABBA mean to you?

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:10.400
Ha-ha, your fault!

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:16.520
To me, ABBA is naturally something -

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.860
- very nice. And -

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:21.540
- the part about them never reuniting ...

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:26.120
Personally, I think that's sad, even if I understand why.

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:27.100
It would have been cool.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:30.300
And it's somewhere ....

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.260
It's hard to answer, I don't really have anything to compare it to.

00:03:33.865 --> 00:03:36.335
You've been asked about this a thousand times, and -

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:38.200
- it's very clear that you don't want to.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.400
But there's one thing I've been wondering about:

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:41.980
Have you had the talk?

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:43.100
If we've had ..?

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:46.180
Have you had the talk about reuniting?

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:47.100
No.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:49.480
Have you had the one meeting where you've sat down and agreed ..?

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:50.480
No.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:52.540
We received an offer ten years ago.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:54.060
Which I don't believe at all.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.300
That we would receive an enormous amount of money -

00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:59.380
- to play 100 concerts in a year.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:01.760
Completely sponsored or what it was.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:05.260
And the amount was so big that we called each other and asked «what do you think?»

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:06.720
And everyone said «nooo».

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.460
So it's quite easy, actually.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:11.920
So it's not like there's one poor person who really wants to do it?

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:13.700
No, it's not like that. No one wants to.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:14.560
You agree?

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:15.660
Yes.

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:17.860
Then it sounds like it won't happen.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:18.540
As of yet.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:22.060
«As of yet»! That's good to hear!

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:23.720
We'll see.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:24.400
We'll see.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:25.340
Can't you push a little?

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:26.780
Sure, I'll do that.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:31.815
Would you be able to ... Do you have another «Mamma Mia» in you?

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:34.480
Would you be able to write «Dancing Queen» today?

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:39.400
I think that pop music is best written by -

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:40.960
- young people -

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:44.440
- when they're at the height of their creativity.

00:04:45.115 --> 00:04:47.415
Every person has a period like that, I think.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:50.800
And that might just last for only ten years.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:54.040
And later you might enter some other form of creativity.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:55.940
But just that peak ...

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:58.560
We had that in the 70's -

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:00.220
- together with ABBA.

00:05:00.895 --> 00:05:02.365
You have got ...

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:04.780
... fans all over the world.

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:09.180
They got in contact when they heard you were coming here.

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:13.840
Does it happen that you connect with someone over time, or -

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.160
- is it just one reply, and then ...

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:17.440
Well, yes ...

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:21.060
There are a few people who I've gotten to know over time -

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:22.935
- and who I keep in touch with.

00:05:22.935 --> 00:05:25.615
There is one special fan. He -

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.640
- got in contact four years ago.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:31.460
And this is a story that really touches me.

00:05:31.465 --> 00:05:34.275
He came from humble circumstances.

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:38.820
He was around 60 when he began writing me.

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:42.620
He trained to be a butcher when he was 16 years old.

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:47.200
And when the foot-and-mouth disease broke out in England in 2002 -

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.540
- he was summoned by the authorities -

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:55.040
- to help butcher all these livestock that had been infected.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:58.440
And he went through a horrible period -

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:03.220
- partly because he was forced to butcher all these animals -

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:05.460
- in a special way, which I'll avoid telling the details about -

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:07.700
- but in addition -

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:13.440
- he took part in the families sorrow over losing their income.

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:18.840
And this resulted in him getting a severe depression.

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:23.620
Where he eventually decided that he would take his own life.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:25.300
And ...

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:28.560
He was on his way to do it.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:31.320
He brought his favorite ABBA tape with him.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:33.480
Which he listened to. And he brought his rifle.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:36.120
And he went out to finish it.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:39.115
Just when he's ready -

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:43.720
- ABBA sings «The Way Old Friends Do».

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:46.440
And he stops and listens to these lyrics.

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:48.735
And he thinks: «This is exactly what life is like».

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:50.120
You have to ...

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.180
Be together with friends and -

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:54.840
- help each other and be there.

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:57.680
Like old friends do to each other.

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:01.920
And he had left his wife without saying anything to her, naturally.

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:04.800
And he had children and grandchildren, he had a big family.

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:06.460
So he changed.

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:09.060
Because of this song.

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:12.260
And he went back to his wife and told her everything.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:16.240
And then I received this letter where he told the whole story to me.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:18.420
And we continued to communicate.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:20.160
And I was, of course, very taken.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.940
So I wrote him a quite long and friendly letter.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:26.480
And after that we stayed in touch.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:29.480
And he wrote me a letter once a month -

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:33.040
- where he told me about his life, his family,  his friends -

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:35.520
- about his hobbies, everything he did.

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:38.260
He was a wonderful writer.

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:40.860
He wrote really, really well.

00:07:41.140 --> 00:07:45.200
Then what happened eventually was that he got lung cancer -

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:48.000
- and he passed away in February this year.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.620
And that was sad.

